WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.107
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.107 --> 00:00:05.401
<v Examiner>Identify the five principles of primary health care,

3
00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:09.773
<v Examiner>and describe how a nurse could apply each one in a community setting.

4
00:00:10.223 --> 00:00:15.371
Primary health care is people-centred, from birth to the end of life, and holistic:

5
00:00:15.371 --> 00:00:17.108
physical, mental and social.

6
00:00:17.108 --> 00:00:20.953
Its five principles, each with what it looks like in practice.

7
00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:22.675
One, accessibility.

8
00:00:22.675 --> 00:00:26.890
Care must reach everyone, whatever their race, faith or income.

9
00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:31.039
In practice: physical access, cost, transport, digital access,

10
00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:33.649
and appointment times that suit people.

11
00:00:34.099 --> 00:00:35.942
Two, community participation.

12
00:00:35.942 --> 00:00:41.345
Go and meet the community, find out what it needs, and let people shape the services.

13
00:00:41.345 --> 00:00:46.621
Lisa's quiz answer for this one: whānau helping to shape health plans and services.

14
00:00:47.071 --> 00:00:48.584
Three, health promotion.

15
00:00:48.584 --> 00:00:54.006
Helping people understand what drives their health and build the skills to improve it:

16
00:00:54.006 --> 00:00:58.293
education, nutrition advice, pamphlets, conversations and campaigns.

17
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:00.628
Four, appropriate technology.

18
00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:06.023
Technology that's sound, fits local needs and is acceptable to the people using it,

19
00:01:06.023 --> 00:01:09.793
like telehealth and online consults for a rural community.

20
00:01:10.243 --> 00:01:12.550
Five, intersectoral collaboration.

21
00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:17.979
Health can't do it alone, so nurses work with other sectors: housing, education,

22
00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:21.507
transport and government agencies, not just doctors.

23
00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:27.566
If the question asks about health promotion instead, Lisa's continuum runs in five steps:

24
00:01:27.566 --> 00:01:32.733
health education, using teach-back; health screening, organised and opportunistic;

25
00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:37.711
community action, starting with a community needs assessment; social marketing,

26
00:01:37.711 --> 00:01:40.546
getting the word out; and influencing policy,

27
00:01:40.546 --> 00:01:43.949
locally by keeping practice in line with the evidence,

28
00:01:43.949 --> 00:01:47.289
and more widely by advocating on things like housing.

29
00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:53.074
Five principles of primary health care: accessibility, community participation,

30
00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:57.802
health promotion, appropriate technology, intersectoral collaboration.

31
00:01:57.802 --> 00:02:03.543
Health promotion continuum: education, screening, community action, social marketing,

32
00:02:03.543 --> 00:02:04.827
influencing policy.
